00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:04.220 align:start position:0%
I think the obvious starting point for  discussing the look of silence is to  start with the act of killing the film  you made before this they're  dramatically different in terms of the  ways that they're made stylistically in  terms of the the people they focus on  and yet obviously there's a kinship  there so we should talk a little bit  about how this piece emerged out of that  one and what the relationship really was  like there well the first there's a  scene in the look of silence which  really inspired the making of both films  and it's the scene where the two men  take me down to the river taking turns  playing victim and perpetrator showing  how they can pretend pretending to be  proud I would emphasize pretending to be  proud of what they did is they take me  to the spot where they help the army  kill ten thousand five hundred people I  and then reveal that one of them killed  my friend's son and brother romley and  that was that I shot that scene in  january 2004 and it was the first time I  I was been shooting already with the  perpetrators for eight months at the  request of Adi his family and the other  survivors who had been threatened by the  army back in 2003 not to participate in  the film they then called me to the  secret nighttime midnight meeting and  said don't give up try to film the  perpetrators I was afraid to do so at  first but when I overcame that fear and  approach them I found this I encountered  this horrific boasting and I spent the  first eight months filming them  one-on-one because I didn't know if it  would be safe to bring two together one  might tell the other you shouldn't talk  about this and we could get in trouble  and the whole thing could stop before we  even really understood what had happened  where it happened how it happened and  but after around eight months I felt I  need to see if these men are only  boasting for me when I'm alone with them  or it's something about me and my camera  or would they talk this way with each  other and I took the risk of bringing  two men together and to film that scene  and I saw that they were even worse when  they were together that some  how they were clearly reading from a  shared script and the boasting therefore  I had to acknowledge is systemic is  political is a symptom of impunity and I  had to relinquish whatever hope I might  have comforting hope that these men are  psychotic or monsters whatever psychosis  or monstrosities is evidenced here is  political as well and collective and I  that that realization came to me  actually through the thought which I had  for the first time when I was walking  down to the river with those two men  that it's as though I've wandered into  Germany 40 years after the Holocaust  only to find the Nazis still in power if  the rest of the world had celebrated the  Holocaust while it was taking place and  I decided I had this awful sense that I  have this that this may not be some  surreal scenario from science fiction  this may not be this weird exception to  the rule it this idea of what if the  Nazis had one it actually actually there  been myriad at numerous massacres across  the global south where there's been no  justice where impunity prevails and  people live in fear and I started to  wonder whether perhaps this impunity is  the story of our times whether perhaps  the idea of what if the Nazis that one  is not the exception but the rule across  much of the world and I decided that  evening I would stop everything else I  was doing all my other projects and  focus on this on this impunity not on  what happened in 1965 1965 for as long  as necessary and I knew that very  evening I noted in my diary I'll make  two films one about the lies the  fantasies the stories the perpetrators  tell themselves so they can live with  themselves and the terrible effects of  those lies when imposed on the whole  society and that of course is the act of  killing and it's a film about escapism  and guilt and so it is a kind of  flamboyant fever dream of a film and I  knew I would make a second film though  which is asking what is it like  especially for the survivors but for any  human being to have to live in that  regime of corruption thuggery fear that  the perpetrators have built what is it  due to  beings to have to live for half a  century in silence and in fear and the  act of killing there's an uncut that a  longer sort of the original uncut  version of the film is out in the US as  the director's cut but it it's the  version that was released outside the US  I only mention it because it's cut  through by these moments of absolute  silence and sort of war type moments  where time stops and it's these moments  where the perspective shifts from the  perpetrators to the absent dead I think  and in the look of silence I had this  feeling that the audience should feel as  though they've entered any one of those  haunted silences that punctuate the act  of killing and feel what would it be  like to have to live there after as a  survivor rebuild a life in the rubble  surrounded by the still powerful men who  killed her loved ones what is it like  never to be able to grieve openly and  therefore never to be able to mourn and  heal and what is it like to what does  that do to a family to a human being to  our capacity to love that's the look of  sight it's a really powerful way of  talking about the symbiotic relationship  with the two films because they both  involve two very different kinds of risk  in the first film I think a lot of  people came out of it wondering why you  wouldn't confront these people about  these atrocities they were boasting  about and that's the essence of this  movie which makes one wonder you know  what were the conditions and under which  that could happen you know and how did  you manage with adi to go about making  that happen well so as I said Adi was  the person who I started this journey  with in 2003 and while I was filming the  perpetrators he would watch everything I  had time to show him and we shot the  look of thought we shot the look of  silence after editing the act of killing  but before it had its first screenings  at which point I knew I couldn't safely  return to Indonesia a couple years  before though we when we finished  editing when we finished shooting sorry  the act of killing I give a Nadia small  camera to use as a kind of visual  notebook for to look for images that  might inspire the making of the second  film and when I came back and while i  was editing the act of killing Adi was  sending me tapes when i returned to  indonesia  after after we finished the editing in  2012 to make the look of silence I  didn't know auti would be the main  character I just knew he would be a main  collaborator still and I sat down with  him and said what do you think we should  do for the second film and Adi said i  spent seven years watching the footage  you've shot with the perpetrators it's  changed me and I need to meet them I  need to go and confront the men who  killed my brother and I said instantly  absolutely not it's too dangerous there  has never been a non fiction film where  survivors confront perpetrators who are  still in power it's simply too dangerous  and Adi took out the carrots had let me  explain why this matters to me and took  out the camera that I'd given him and  took out a tape and he said I'm sorry  but I never sent you this tape because  it's very personal to me and he put it  in the trembling he put the tape in the  camera press play and as soon as the  image appeared on the the footage  appeared that he shot on the flip screen  of the camera before I could even really  see what it was he started to cry and  what and he proceeded to show me the one  seen in the look of silence that Adi  shot and it's the scene where his father  at the end is crawling through the house  lost calling for help and he said Joshua  this was the first day my father  couldn't remember me my brother's my  sister's my mother and he was calling  for help lost confused all day and when  we would try to help him we were  strangers to him and he would only grow  more afraid and it was unbearable to sit  there watch it waiting for him to get  better not being able to do anything and  at some point not knowing what else to  do i picked up the camera and then  filmed i had it he said because he had  it because it was a sort of family  reunion and while filming he said the  moment i started to film i understood  why i was filming i was filming because  this is the day that it becomes too late  for my father to heal he's forgotten the  Sun whose murder destroyed his life  destroyed our family's life but he  hasn't forgotten the fear and now he  will never be able to  work through that fear and he'll because  he can't remember what happened and so  and so I feel like he said my father's  like is now trapped in this prison of  fear like a man locked in a room who  can't remember even where the door is  let alone where he's put the key and  then we watched the scene in silence as  it finished and at the end he looked at  me and said Josh Joshua I do not want my  children to inherit this prison of fear  from my father my mother and from me and  I think if i visit the perpetrators  they'll see that i'm not there for  revenge I'm not there out of anger and  they'll greet this as an opportunity to  make peace with their neighbors to find  forgiveness from one of their victims  relatives and they will acknowledge what  they did was wrong and I'll forgive them  and in that way finally we will be able  to live with our neighbors no longer  afraid but as human beings
 

